    "case_summary": "A full-term girl presented at birth with multiple skin lesions. One month later, she returned due to recurring skin eruptions. She was born to healthy, nonconsanguineous parents. Growth and development were normal for her age and sex. The patient's family medical history was unremarkable. At 3-year follow-up, the vesicles and bullae began decreasing at 6 months old but intermittently returned after 1 year of age.\n\nInitial exam: Multiple areas of erythema, vesicles, and bullae on the face, trunk, and extremities. Vital signs including temperature, respiration, and heart rate were within normal ranges. Follow-up exam: Multiple round or irregularly shaped erythematous macules and slightly scaly plaques of various sizes on face, trunk, and extremities. Bullous and vesicular lesions on thighs, with scattered facial erosions. Mucous membranes not affected. Later developed thickened, brown, corrugated plaques over neck, armpits, wrists, and ankles, along with palmoplantar hyperkeratosis.\n\n- Skin-punch biopsy: Revealed hyperkeratosis, distinct vacuolization in the granular and upper spinous layers, and bullae formation within the epidermis, with sparse inflammatory cells in the superficial dermis.- Direct immunofluorescence (DIF): Results were unremarkable.- Genetic testing: Whole-exome sequencing revealed a heterozygous variation c.482T>C (p. L161P) in KRT1.- Images:  Figure 1A Title: Hyperkeratotic plaques  Figure 1A Description: Hyperkeratotic plaques with distinct borders and annular appearance    Figure 1B Title: Histopathology of erythematous scaly plaque  Figure 1B Description: Shows hyperkeratosis, spinous cell vacuolization, and vesicles in the epidermis    Figure 2 Title: Histopathological examination  Figure 2 Description: Hematoxylin-eosin staining, original magnification ×100\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Epidermolytic ichthyosis (bullous congenital ichthyosiform erythroderma)\nB. Superficial epidermolytic ichthyosis\nC. Ichthyosis bullosa of Siemens\nD. Annular epidermolytic ichthyosis (AEI)\n",
